---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Plate from "../components/Plate.astro";
import "../styles/drawing.css";
import { computeHouse, TYPOLOGIES, TYPOLOGY_ORDER } from "../lib/house.js";
import { renderProfile } from "../lib/draw.js";

const houses = TYPOLOGY_ORDER.map((k) => {
  const t = TYPOLOGIES[k];
  const h = computeHouse({
    typology: k,
    widthKen: t.ken.w[2],
    depthKen: t.ken.d[2],
    exposure: k === "gyoka" ? "coastal" : k === "gassho" ? "snow" : "inland",
  });
  return { k, t, h, profile: renderProfile(h) };
});
---

<Layout
  title="Ibushi 燻 — minka raised, moved, and revived | Hida, Gifu"
  description="A workshop in Hida building traditional Japanese villager houses — nōka, gyoka, machiya, and gasshō-zukuri — on the old methods. A minka is cured by the smoke of being lived in."
  nav=""
>
  <!-- ================= hero ================= -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-media">
      <Plate
        name="hero-smoke"
        alt="A thatched farmhouse at first light across a frosted rice field, one straight column of woodsmoke standing off the ridge into cold blue air, mountains behind."
        ratio="16 / 9"
        eager={true}
      />
    </div>
    <div class="wrap hero-text">
      <h1>A minka is cured<br />by the smoke of<br />being lived in.</h1>
      <div class="hero-cols">
        <p>
          There is no chimney. The fire in the floor sends its smoke up through
          the open frame and out through the thatch, and on the way it lays
          soot on every beam and every stem of straw it passes. That soot kills
          the insects, drives out the damp, and stops the rot.
        </p>
        <p class="spec gloss">
          Which means the building is preserved by being inhabited. A house
          whose fire goes out begins to fail — not sentimentally, structurally.
          We raise, move, and revive these houses in Hida, and we ask every
          client the same question before anything else: will the fire be lit?
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the argument ================= -->
  <section class="argument">
    <div class="wrap arg-grid">
      <div class="arg-media">
        <Plate
          name="susu-beam"
          alt="Extreme close detail of two hundred years of hearth soot lacquered onto a massive zelkova beam, the adze-cut scallops still legible beneath, raking daylight across the surface."
          ratio="4 / 5"
          caption="Two hundred years of soot on a keyaki tie-beam. The adze marks are still legible under it."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="arg-text">
        <span class="kicker">Why the smoke matters · 煤</span>
        <h2>Soot is a finish, not a stain.</h2>
        <p>
          A beam over a working irori takes on a hard, near-black lacquer that
          you cannot buy and cannot fake. It takes decades. Insects will not
          bore it, water will not sit on it, and the fungi that destroy
          untreated timber cannot get a hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same smoke rises into the roof and hardens the thatch from
          beneath, which is why a smoked kayabuki roof lasts three or four
          times as long as one over a house that stands empty. It is also why
          the bamboo taken out of an old roof at re-thatch — <span class="term"
            >susutake</span
          > — is worth more than the bamboo that went in.
        </p>
        <p class="spec gloss">
          The trade-off is real and we will not pretend otherwise: an open
          hearth means smoke in the room, and a house run this way is warmer,
          darker at the ceiling, and needs its fire kept. Clients who want the
          look without the fire get a building that will need us again in
          thirty years.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the four houses ================= -->
  <section class="four">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">The four houses · 四つの民家</span>
      <h2>One valley, four answers.</h2>
      <p class="four-lede">
        <span class="term">Minka</span> 民家 means, plainly, "the houses of the
        people" — everyone who was not a samurai or a priest. It is not one
        building type. It is four, and the differences between them are not
        style: they are answers to four different climates and four different
        ways of earning a living. From this workshop all four are within a
        morning's drive.
      </p>

      <ul class="four-list">
        {
          houses.map(({ k, t, h, profile }) => (
            <li class="four-item">
              <a href={`/houses/${k}`}>
                <div class="four-profile" set:html={profile} />
                <h3>
                  {t.label} <span class="jp four-jp">{t.jp}</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="four-gloss spec">{t.gloss}</p>
                <p class="four-line">{t.line}</p>
                <dl class="four-spec">
                  <div>
                    <dt>Kōbai</dt>
                    <dd>
                      {Math.round(h.kobai * 10) / 10} sun · {Math.round(h.deg)}°
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <dt>Roof</dt>
                    <dd>{h.thatch > 0 ? "Kaya thatch" : "Kawara tile"}</dd>
                  </div>
                </dl>
                <span class="four-go spec">Read the house →</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the signature ================= -->
  <section class="sig">
    <div class="wrap sig-grid">
      <div class="sig-text">
        <span class="kicker">Sumitsuke · 墨付け</span>
        <h2>Nothing is cut until the line is drawn.</h2>
        <p>
          Before a joint is cut, the <span class="term">tōryō</span> walks the
          stacked timber with an ink pot and a chalked cord and snaps a
          dead-straight line onto every crooked log. That marking is called <span class="term">sumitsuke</span>, and it decides the house.
        </p>
        <p>
          Snap yours. Choose the house, the bays in <span class="term">ken</span
          >, the weather the site actually gets, and how much of the ground stays
          earth — and the section and plan redraw from those numbers alone, at a
          real drafting scale that changes as the house does. The specification
          it produces is what we would build from.
        </p>
        <a class="btn" href="/sumitsuke">Snap a line</a>
      </div>
      <div class="sig-media">
        <Plate
          name="toryo"
          alt="A master carpenter in his sixties crouched over a squared timber, snapping a straight ink line onto the wood with a traditional ink pot and cord, his face lit by daylight from a large open doorway."
          ratio="4 / 5"
          caption="Ōno Sōhachi marking a tie-beam. Sixth of his line to hold the title of tōryō."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <!-- ================= the frame + the record ================= -->
  <section class="ends">
    <div class="wrap ends-grid">
      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="magari-bari"
          alt="Looking up into a roof frame: enormous naturally curved pine beams left in the shape the tree grew, crossing overhead, joined without nails and blackened by hearth smoke."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The frame</h3>
        <p>
          Posts standing loose on stones so the house can ride an earthquake.
          Beams left in the curve the tree grew. Joints cut to hold without a
          single nail — because a nail rusts, and a joint does not.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/frame">Kigumi, ishiba-date, and the great pillar →</a>
      </article>

      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="tatami-shoji"
          alt="A tatami-matted room in the morning, paper screens glowing with low sun and casting their lattice across the mats, a timber-framed panel half open to a further room."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The rooms</h3>
        <p>
          Earth, then a step, then board, then matting. The house gets softer,
          quieter, and more formal the deeper you walk, and nobody has to be
          told where they may go.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/rooms">Doma to engawa, in order →</a>
      </article>

      <article class="end">
        <Plate
          name="yui-rethatch"
          alt="About twenty villagers on the steep thatched roof of a farmhouse, passing up bundles of golden miscanthus and combing the new thatch flush, old grey thatch stripped from one half."
          ratio="3 / 2"
        />
        <h3>The record</h3>
        <p>
          Every house we have raised, moved, or revived, entered as a <span class="term">munafuda</span> — the tag nailed inside the ridge
          naming who built it, for whom, and when.
        </p>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/munafuda">The record since 1968 →</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="snap" />

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap cta-in">
      <div>
        <h2>Will the fire be lit?</h2>
        <p>
          It is the first thing we ask. Tell us the site and the weather, and we
          will tell you plainly whether we are the right workshop for it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn" href="/commission">Begin a commission</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* --- hero -------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .hero {
    padding-block: 0 clamp(3rem, 6vw, 6rem);
  }

  .hero-media :global(.plate img),
  .hero-media :global(.plate-hold) {
    max-height: 74vh;
  }

  .hero-text {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.25rem);
  }

  .hero-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(19rem, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 62rem;
  }

  /* --- argument ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .argument {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 7rem);
  }

  .arg-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.85fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .arg-text h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  /* --- the four ---------------------------------------------------------- */

  .four {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 7rem);
  }

  .four h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .four-lede {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-bottom: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .four-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--susu);
  }

  .four-item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--susu);
    border-right: 1px solid var(--susu);
  }

  .four-item:last-child {
    border-right: 0;
  }

  .four-item a {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    height: 100%;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.35rem 1.6rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background 160ms ease;
  }

  .four-item a:hover {
    background: var(--washi-lit);
  }

  .four-profile {
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }

  .four-item h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }

  .four-jp {
    font-size: 0.62em;
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
    margin-left: 0.35rem;
  }

  .four-gloss {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.9rem;
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
  }

  .four-line {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
  }

  .four-spec {
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--sugi) 30%, transparent);
  }

  .four-spec > div {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.35rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--sugi) 20%, transparent);
  }

  .four-spec dt,
  .four-spec dd {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .four-spec dt {
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
  }

  .four-go {
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
    margin-top: auto;
  }

  .four-item a:hover .four-go {
    color: var(--okibi);
  }

  /* --- signature --------------------------------------------------------- */

  .sig {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 7rem);
  }

  .sig-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.8fr);
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .sig-text h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .sig-text .btn {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* --- ends -------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .ends {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 6rem);
  }

  .ends-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .end h3 {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0.7rem;
  }

  .end p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .quiet-link {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--sugi-deep);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--okibi);
    padding-bottom: 2px;
  }

  .quiet-link:hover {
    color: var(--susu);
  }

  /* --- cta --------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .cta {
    padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 5.5rem);
  }

  .cta-in {
    display: flex;
    align-items: end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 2rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .cta p {
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    max-width: 32rem;
  }

  /* --- mobile ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  @media (max-width: 62rem) {
    .arg-grid,
    .sig-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .arg-media,
    .sig-media {
      order: -1;
    }
    .four-item {
      border-right: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
